WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.133
The from-scratch builds.

2
00:00:02.433 --> 00:00:08.433
Feynman's rule, the core of Karpathy's teaching, and the fastest route from practitioner to architect.

3
00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:11.100
Ten builds, done in order.

4
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:17.259
Each takes a weekend to a week, and each one permanently removes a category of confusion.

5
00:00:17.259 --> 00:00:19.300
Each is also a portfolio piece.

6
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.733
The first five builds go inside the model.

7
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:27.497
Micrograd: an autograd engine in about a hundred and fifty lines.

8
00:00:27.497 --> 00:00:29.900
Backprop is never mysterious again.

9
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.067
A byte pair tokenizer, which explains token counts and costs.

10
00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:38.500
Then nanoGPT, a working transformer trained on your own text.

11
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.867
Add multi-head attention, causal masking, RoPE, and a KV cache.

12
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:46.733
Finally, LoRA by hand, then compare against peft.

13
00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:49.867
The next five build real systems.

14
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:53.200
A RAG with no framework, in about two hundred lines.

15
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:58.033
An agent loop from zero, then read LangGraph and recognize every piece.

16
00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:01.067
Your own eval harness, before you touch RAGAS.

17
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:05.833
An MCP server on real data, the highest leverage item on the list.

18
00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:07.544
And a serving benchmark.

19
00:01:07.544 --> 00:01:09.367
Numbers you own beat any claim.

20
00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:13.000
So why does building work when reading doesn't?

21
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.455
Reading lets you accept a hand-wave.

22
00:01:15.455 --> 00:01:16.233
Code doesn't.

23
00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:18.933
Code makes you ask what shape that tensor is.

24
00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:20.900
And why the loss is not moving.

25
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.333
That is the mechanism by which understanding forms.

26
00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:27.000
Here is where it pays off.

27
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.933
In an interview, you say: I built it from scratch.

28
00:01:30.233 --> 00:01:32.000
They can probe you for ten minutes.

29
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.433
And it holds, which makes it the most credible line you have.

30
00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:37.264
Let's recap.

31
00:01:37.264 --> 00:01:39.733
If you can't build it, you don't understand it.

32
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.533
Do the ten builds in order.

33
00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:44.500
Code forces answers that reading lets you skip.

34
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.642
And built from scratch holds up in interviews.

35
00:01:47.642 --> 00:01:49.433
Next up: how top minds think.
